---
import ArticleLayout from '../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import StoreButtons from '../components/StoreButtons.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/comparisons';

const path = '/from-tv-time';
const title = 'TV Time alternatives: where to track your shows now';
const description =
  'TV Time closed on 15 July 2026. An honest guide to the replacements: Simkl and Trakt for automatic tracking, Serializd for TV reviews, and I Like Movies for episode tracking with a shared household library, which imports a TV Time export you saved.';

// Every recommendation here that is not the author's own app is deliberate.
// A page for displaced TV Time users that only offers its own product would
// be the least trustworthy document on this subject on the internet. Naming
// the apps that fit other needs better is what makes the one paragraph about
// this app credible.

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can I import my TV Time history into another app?',
    a: [
      'Yes, if you exported it before the shutdown. TV Time closed on 15 July 2026 and user data was deleted, so that export file is the only copy left. I Like Movies, which is written by the author of this page, imports it, and other replacement apps may read it too, so check before you choose. If you did not export, no app can recover the history, and the honest move is to rebuild progress as you go.',
    ],
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the closest replacement for TV Time?',
    a: [
      'It depends on what you used it for. For automatic tracking, Simkl or Trakt. For public reviews and a TV community, Serializd. For episode tracking on your phone with films in the same library and watchlists your household can see, I Like Movies, which is written by the author of this page.',
    ],
  },
  {
    q: 'Does I Like Movies import TV Time data?',
    a: [
      'Yes, from the data export TV Time sent you before it closed. Upload the .zip in the app under Imports, or, if the .zip asks for a password, the file tracking-prod-records-v2.csv from inside it. Watched episodes, watched films, film ratings, your film watchlist and your lists come across, and you see what was found before anything is added.',
      'Episode ratings and shows you followed but never started are not imported, films are matched by title and year, and the .csv on its own brings watched episodes only. Without an export there is nothing to import: series progress is set as you go, and marking a whole season watched takes a tap.',
    ],
  },
  {
    q: 'Why did TV Time shut down?',
    a: [
      'TV Time announced its closure and went offline on 15 July 2026. This page does not speculate beyond the public facts: the service closed and accounts were deleted. What matters for a viewer is the practical part, which is choosing where the tracking habit goes next.',
    ],
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
    mainEntityOfPage: new URL(path, SITE.url).href,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((faq) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: faq.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: faq.a.join(' ') },
    })),
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={`${title} | I Like Movies`}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  heading="TV Time alternatives after the shutdown"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    TV Time shut down on 15 July 2026, and years of episode check-ins went with
    it. If you are reading this, you probably kept a habit alive in that app
    for a long time and would like somewhere sensible to continue it. This
    page is the short, honest version of the options.
  </p>

  <div class="plain-panel">
    <p>
      Before anything else: <strong>bring your TV Time export if you saved one.</strong>
      TV Time's data was deleted when the service closed, so no app can fetch
      your history now, but the export file it sent you before the shutdown
      still works. I Like Movies reads it: the .zip, or the file
      tracking-prod-records-v2.csv from inside it if the .zip asks for a
      password. If you never exported, there is nothing to import. A
      disappointed install is worse than none, so this is said up front.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>If automatic tracking was the point</h2>
  <p>
    TV Time asked you to check in. If what you actually want next is an app
    that notices your watching by itself, two services do that properly.
    <strong>Trakt</strong> is the ecosystem choice: media servers and players
    like Plex and Kodi scrobble to it automatically, and dozens of apps build
    on its API. <strong>Simkl</strong> gets there without a media server, using
    browser extensions that log what you watch on streaming sites, and it is
    also the strongest of the three for anime. Both are free with ads and sell
    premium tiers.
  </p>

  <h2>If the reviews and the community were the point</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Serializd</strong> is built around TV reviews and discussion, the
    way Letterboxd is built around film reviews. If reading and writing about
    shows was your favourite part of TV Time, that is the culture it continues.
  </p>

  <h2>If episode tracking on your phone was the point</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={SITE.url}>I Like Movies</a> is written by the author of this page,
    so weigh this paragraph accordingly. It tracks TV at episode level, marks a
    season in a tap, and can tell a series that has ended from one between
    seasons. Films live in the same library rather than in a second app, every
    title shows where it streams in your country, an AI assistant answers
    "what should we start next" in plain language, and a household can see each
    other's watchlists, with a shared collection and a group chat. It is
    free, has no ads, and runs on iPhone and Android. There is no automatic
    tracking, no web app, and no public reviews; if any
    of those is essential, the apps above fit better.
  </p>

  <h2>With or without an export</h2>
  <p>
    With an export, upload it under Imports in the app. You see what was found
    before anything is added, and the whole import can be undone within a day
    if it is not what you expected. Shows you were part way through come
    across as watched episodes, but an import never adds a show to Watching,
    so add the few you are still following from each show's page.
  </p>
  <p>
    Without one, rebuilding sounds worse than it is. You do not need your
    history back to continue the habit; you need current-season positions for
    the shows you are actually mid-way through, which for most people is fewer
    than ten. Mark those seasons, and let the back catalogue fill in whenever a
    rewatch happens to touch it. Two minutes of setup, not an afternoon of data
    entry.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  {
    faqs.map((faq) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{faq.q}</summary>
        {faq.a.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <p>
    Every option side by side:
    <a href="/best/tv-show-tracker-apps">the best TV show tracker apps in 2026</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    More reading: <a href="/guides">every guide, watch order and comparison</a>
    on this site, including the full <a href="/vs/trakt">Trakt</a>,{' '}
    <a href="/vs/simkl">Simkl</a> and <a href="/vs/serializd">Serializd</a>{' '}
    comparisons.
  </p>

  <div class="cta-row">
    <StoreButtons />
    <a href="/">More about the app</a>
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
